Doug Freyburger wrote:
> Ray Kulhanek <kulhane...@wright.edu> wrote:
>> Not all the conducts would need to be included.  Wishless can't
>> really be broken accidentally,
> 
> The macro in the code to check for the first violation of a conduct
> would likely be easier if it checked for all conducts.  In the case of
> a wish it might be nice to throw away the wish at a conduct and
> drop the code that inspects the string to see if you didn't want to
> violate the conduct.
As a macro, yes.  but how about something like this:
int check_conduct(char type) {
   if (!strchr(conduct_checklist, type)) return 1;
   switch (type) {
     case 'w':
       return ('y' == yn(
         "This would violate weaponless conduct. Confirm action?"));
     case 'v':
       ...
     default:
       /* Can't happen. Log error */
   }
}

Admittedly, that could be done as a macro, but it'd be hideous.

The only problem here is that the u.uconduct increments generally
happen after the action, so each of the 95 instances of u.uconduct
would need to be examined and the place where the conduct is actually
violated would need to be found so the function could be called before
that point.  But that's true of the macro as well, and the difference
in speed isn't an issue since it'd only be called during user input,
so it won't be a bottleneck.

>> and most of the ways of breaking
>> polyfooless would be due to traps
> 
> Traps are a part of why some conducts are challenging but plenty
> of players do manage to avoid them.

Yes, and if they avoid them, the prompt wouldn't appear because they
weren't about to violate the conduct.  And if they didn't, the prompt
wouldn't appear because they don't get a choice.  What I meant was
that I couldn't think of a way in which a player could accidentally
polymorph themselves without zapping an unidentified wand, quaffing
an unidentified potion, wearing an unidentified ring, or stepping on
a trap.  And none of those should give a prompt since the player is
taking a risk by trying it in the first place.


